<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Carmel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Carmel is a coastal mountain range in northern Israel, rising above the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Long held sacred, it is linked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> with the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hermits settled there in the 12th century, and the Carmelite order of friars took shape among them in the early 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-carmel.jpg" alt="Mount Carmel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chadner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caiobadner_-_mount_carmel.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Mount Carmel is used for three areas: the whole range of about 39 km that runs to Jenin, its northwestern 19 km, or the headland at the northwestern end. The range is 6.5 to 8 kilometres wide and slopes gradually to the southwest, while its northeastern face forms a steep ridge 546 metres high. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezreel-valley">Jezreel Valley</a> lies immediately to the northeast. The range is a natural barrier, as the valley is a natural passageway, and together they have shaped migration and invasion through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between 1930 and 1932 Dorothy Garrod excavated caves and rock shelters at el-Wad, el-Tabun and Es Skhul, finding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthal</a> and early modern human remains, among them the Neanderthal female known as Tabun I. The Tabun cave has the longest stratigraphic record in the region, spanning 600,000 years or more of human activity. In Roman and Byzantine times a belt of Jewish villages flourished across the range, including Horvat Sumaqa, with its monumental synagogue of the 3rd and 4th centuries CE.</p><p>The mountain was held sacred in antiquity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> lists a holy headland among his Canaanite territories, and if this is Carmel, as some Egyptologists believe, the mountain was sacred from at least the 15th century BCE. A Greek inscription published by Michael Avi-Yonah in 1952 is dedicated to Heliopolitan Zeus of Carmel, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> states that an oracle there was consulted by Vespasian.</p><p>In the Books of Kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> challenges 450 prophets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> to a contest at an altar on the mountain, to show which deity truly ruled Israel. When the prophets of Baal failed to light their sacrifice, Elijah had water poured over his own and prayed, and fire consumed it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> venerate Elijah, and their towns of Daliyat al-Karmel and Isfiya stand on the eastern slopes.</p><p>A Catholic order, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelites</a>, was founded on the mountain in 1209 and named for it. Its founder is unknown; the early Rule, given by Albert, the Latin Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, calls him only Brother B. The monastery, dedicated to Mary as Star of the Sea, often changed hands during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>, was made a hospital by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1799 and was destroyed by the pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1821.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> lies on the northern and western slopes, and the range is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve. The caves of Tabun, Jamal, el-Wad and Skhul were added to the World Heritage List in 2012. The mountain is also sacred to the Baháʼí Faith: the remains of the Báb were laid to rest there on 21 March 1909, and the Shrine of the Báb and the Baháʼí World Centre stand on its slope.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Carmel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Shrine of Bahá’u’lláh at Bahjí, towards which Bahá’ís turn in prayer, and the Shrine of the Báb on <b>Mount Carmel</b>, surrounded by terraced gardens, receive pilgrims from around the world and were inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 2008.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In Raqefet Cave on <b>Mount Carmel</b>, residues of about 13,000 years ago point to possible beer-brewing, although they may come from spontaneous fermentation.</blockquote><a href="/culture/natufians">Natufians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Haifa was a modest lordship with a harbour and the farmland of the <b>Carmel</b> coast, and was at times attached to the Principality of Galilee.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-haifa">Lordship of Haifa</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Middle Bronze Age kingdom, rebuilt with massive ramparts and a palace in the early 2nd millennium BCE, controlled the route through the <b>Carmel</b> ridge along which armies and caravans travelled between Egypt and Syria.</blockquote><a href="/state/megiddo">Megiddo</a></li></ul></section>
